  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Factor $9{w}^{2}+16.$",
  "worked_solution": "To factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{w}^{2}+16 & =9{w}^{2}-(-16) \\\\  & =9{w}^{2}-(16{\\text{i}}^{2}) \\\\  & =(3w)^{2}-{(4\\text{i})}^{2} \\\\  & =(3w+4\\text{i})(3w-4\\text{i}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
